13
00:00:20.343 --> 00:00:21.176
Now I want a flower.

14
00:00:21.176 --> 00:00:22.863
Thanks a lot.
Of course.

15
00:00:22.863 --> 00:00:23.850
You have a big sunflower, look.

16
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.610
Oh I do, (indistinct), perfect!

17
00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:26.443
(audience laughs)

18
00:00:26.443 --> 00:00:29.170
You have the biggest flower of us all.

19
00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:30.469
Thanks for noticing.

20
00:00:30.469 --> 00:00:31.780
(audience laughs)

21
00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:32.959
Ben, this was the first time

22
00:00:32.959 --> 00:00:35.310
you were actually playing a father

23
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.060
and you're not a father in real life.

24
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.110
Tell me about that 'cause I have felt the same way.

25
00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:40.943
It was fun.

26
00:00:40.943 --> 00:00:41.776
Look at that kid,

27
00:00:41.776 --> 00:00:42.921
look how, that's Matilda Lawler

28
00:00:42.921 --> 00:00:46.200
who was on Broadway before she did this movie,

29
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.033
and she's incredible

30
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:48.650
so it made the whole process amazing.

31
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:49.700
First day I like,

32
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.030
I took Matilda and threw her really high in the air.

33
00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:53.360
She held onto the rafters

34
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.006 line:15%
and was like what am I doing?

35
00:00:55.006 --> 00:00:55.839 line:15%
(Ross laughs)

36
00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:57.080 line:15%
I did all the wrong dad things.

37
00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.180 line:15%
We were, it was terrible,

38
00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.120 line:15%
but then we got a hang of it,

39
00:00:59.120 --> 00:00:59.953
and it was great.

40
00:00:59.953 --> 00:01:02.780
All right, Ben, will you do some headlines with us?

41
00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:03.620
I can't wait.

42
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:04.630
I don't know what's about to happen,

43
00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.040
and I'm so thrilled.

44
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:06.873
I can't wait.

45
00:01:06.873 --> 00:01:07.820
We're gonna start with one

46
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:09.620
that I think you'll be able to get behind.

47
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.060
All right, The Boston Globe reports

48
00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.360
that typewriters are making a comeback.

49
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.250
Business is booming for the little shop

50
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.290
called Cambridge Typewriter,

51
00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.330
and sales are up by 40%,

52
00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.136 line:15%
and a lot of younger people are coming in

53
00:01:22.136 --> 00:01:25.190 line:15%
looking for an old clickety clacker.

54
00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.840 line:15%
Ben, what are your thoughts?

55
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.180 line:15%
This is gonna blow everything,

56
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.680 line:15%
but I'm not really at a news desk.

57
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.550 line:15%
I have a series.

58
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.377 line:15%
I have two different typewriters.

59
00:01:34.377 --> 00:01:36.500
I love typewriters.

60
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.080
It's like one of my favorite things.

61
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.200
Do you actually use them like when you write?

62
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.567
I probably use it maybe twice a week.

63
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:45.880
I send my friends letters

64
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.645
and I pretend I'm either them from the past

65
00:01:47.645 --> 00:01:49.710
that sent them a letter from like

66
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.067
or it's their grandparents,

67
00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:53.000
or if my friend talks trash

68
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.605
about like an NBA player on Twitter,

69
00:01:54.605 --> 00:01:56.940
I'll pretend I'm that NBA player,

70
00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.580
and LeBron James will send that person a note

71
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.747
and be like "How dare you make fun of me on Twitter?"

72
00:02:00.747 --> 00:02:02.607
And stuff like that so it's been very.

73
00:02:02.607 --> 00:02:04.340
Okay, not to be pushy,

74
00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:05.730
but maybe we could be pen pals.

75
00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:09.380
I wanna hear about my past and I like writing letters.

76
00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.220
I'm a big fan.
Oh, I cannot wait.

77
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.053
You give me an address.

78
00:02:12.053 --> 00:02:13.840
You will get a letter ASAP.

79
00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.010
I can't wait till you get a letter from him.

80
00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.310
I know, me too.

81
00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.617
I hope it was okay I just was like,

82
00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:19.220
"Ben, we're gonna write each other."

83
00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.260
It's gonna be great.

84
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:21.093
It's good.

85
00:02:21.093 --> 00:02:22.060
Well, this is exciting

86
00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.610
because next we're talking peanut butter passion

87
00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:25.750
in today's food news.

88
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:26.640
Okay.

89
00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.473
(Narrator hums)

90
00:02:27.473 --> 00:02:29.780
Delish reports that a recent survey asked people

91
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.321
if they are a creamy peanut butter person

92
00:02:32.321 --> 00:02:36.310
or a crunchy peanut butter person,

93
00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.430
and it was split right down the middle 50/50

94
00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:39.989
but people were so passionate

95
00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:42.481 line:15%
that half of them said they wouldn't even date someone

96
00:02:42.481 --> 00:02:44.160 line:15%
who liked the other kind,

97
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.230 line:15%
and to that, I say, come on.

98
00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.500 line:15%
The poll also looked at peanut butter personalities.

99
00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.630 line:15%
Listen to this, creamy types are early birds

100
00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:53.980 line:15%
and more introverted,

101
00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:57.088 line:15%
and crunchies are extroverted night owls.

102
00:02:57.088 --> 00:02:59.960 line:15%
Well, I feel very about peanut butter.

103
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:00.793 line:15%
I love it,

104
00:03:00.793 --> 00:03:02.810 line:15%
and I buy creamy because that's just what I know,

105
00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:06.210
but whenever I have crunchy, I feel like such a bad boy,

106
00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.069
you know what I mean?

107
00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:07.902 line:15%
(Drew laughs)

108
00:03:07.902 --> 00:03:08.735 line:15%
Yeah.
Right?

109
00:03:08.735 --> 00:03:10.505 line:15%
You never put a jacket to go out at night.

110
00:03:10.505 --> 00:03:11.673 line:15%
(Ross laughs)

111
00:03:11.673 --> 00:03:13.770 line:15%
Can I also give a follow-up question to you guys?

112
00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:15.430 line:15%
What are your thoughts on tiny spoons?

113
00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.300 line:15%
I love a tiny spoon.

114
00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.440 line:15%
Love a tiny spoon.

115
00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.283
I think about this literally every day,

116
00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:22.350
because I do my yogurt parfait in the morning

117
00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:24.190
and I have this one tiny spoon, one of them,

118
00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.517
and I will have to wash it every single morning

119
00:03:26.517 --> 00:03:29.150
because it's my only tiny spoon.

120
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.290
You gotta buy another one, Ross.

121
00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:31.123
You earned it.

122
00:03:31.123 --> 00:03:31.956
You deserve it.

123
00:03:31.956 --> 00:03:33.890
Here's a tip, don't eat peanut butter on television.

124
00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:35.864
(audience laughs)

125
00:03:35.864 --> 00:03:36.697
It's the best.

126
00:03:36.697 --> 00:03:37.940
Ben, are you crunchy or creamy?

127
00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.212
Are you nice or naughty?

128
00:03:39.212 --> 00:03:40.493
I can do both.

129
00:03:40.493 --> 00:03:41.800
I love both.

130
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.820
Sometimes, one of my things when I'm filming,

131
00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.060
if my energy is getting low,

132
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.440
is I get a peanut butter and jelly sandwich

133
00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:47.467
on a whole wheat,

134
00:03:47.467 --> 00:03:50.330 line:15%
and I usually use creamy for that.

135
00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.030 line:15%
Yeah, I think for a sandwich,

136
00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.800 line:15%
it's too much combativeness with the bread,

137
00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.690
you need creamy,

138
00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:58.289
but I just bought a jar.

139
00:03:58.289 --> 00:03:59.940
You cover your mouth when you chew,

140
00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.740
